2. The `ElectricCar` class extends the `Vehicle` class, meaning it inherits all the properties and methods of the `Vehicle` class, including the `start()`, `drive()`, and `charge()` methods.
3. In the `main()` method of the `Test` class, an instance of the `ElectricCar` class is created and assigned to the `Vehicle` object `v`.
4. The `start()`, `drive()`, and `charge()` methods are called on the `v` object, which are actually instances of the `ElectricCar` class, due to the inheritance relationship between the two classes.

This option is correct because it correctly demonstrates polymorphism in Java, where the `ElectricCar` class is treated as a `Vehicle` object, and the correct methods are called based on the actual type of the object.

